    <DataNode elementId="Glucosyl-ceramides" textLabel="a glucosylceramide" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="36500" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Cerebrosides consist of |FRAME: Ceramides| with a single sugar residue at the 1-hydroxyl moiety. The sugar residue can be either glucose or galactose, and the two major types are thus named |FRAME: Glucosyl-ceramides glucocerebrosides| and |FRAME: D-galactosylceramide galactocerebrosides|. Galactocerebrosides are typically found in neural tissue, while glucocerebrosides are found in other tissues. Based on the notation GlcCer(m:n)-p used to name subclasses of glucosylceramides, the total number of carbons present in the sphingoid base and the fatty amide is &quot;m&quot; while the total number of double bonds is &quot;n.&quot; The total number of hydroxyl groups (not including the hydroxyl group used as the site of the head group attachment) is &quot;p.&quot; The length, hydroxylation position, double bond position, and double bond orientation of the groups attached at R1 and R2 will differ between individual members of this group of compounds.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="Glucosyl-ceramides" />
